# Human-in-the-Loop Escalation Matrix

| Trigger | Source Agent | Human Role | SLA | Required Human Action |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Confidence below 75% or missing order ID | Intake Triage | Returns clerk | 4 business hours | Validate identifiers and complete missing information |
| Expired return window | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Returns supervisor | Same business day | Review policy exception eligibility and rationale |
| Serial/SKU mismatch | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Returns supervisor | Same business day | Verify identity, order, and physical-item evidence |
| Duplicate return request | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Returns supervisor | Same business day | Determine duplicate, fraud, or valid separate issue |
| Prompt-injection signal | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Returns supervisor | Same business day | Review evidence; preserve event for security analysis |
| High-value item | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Operations manager | 4 business hours | Conduct enhanced review before any customer-impacting decision |
| Repeat-return threshold | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Operations manager | 4 business hours | Assess pattern with context; avoid automatic penalty |
| Conflicting records | Fraud and Policy Analyst | Operations manager | 4 business hours | Reconcile order, refund, shipment, and return history |
| Invalid queue match / duplicate assignment | Routing Coordinator | Returns supervisor | 4 business hours | Correct routing and confirm case ownership |

## Meaningful HITL requirement

A human reviewer must:

1. Open the underlying order and return records.
2. Check the policy version cited by the agent.
3. Confirm or reject the agent’s risk rationale.
4. Record an independent reason for any final decision.
5. Avoid relying on the agent’s classification as the sole basis for action.

Human approval rate and override patterns should be audited to detect rubber-stamp behavior.
